{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Do not let your hair grow disheveled… and upon all the congregation…</b> Meaning: if they [the rest of Israel] act as you do — for it is specifically you who are subject to this prohibition — but upon all the congregation there would be wrath if they did so; rather, they are obligated to tear [their garments] over them. Or perhaps He judged concerning them the law of the head of a court, regarding whom all of Israel is obligated to tear (Mo'ed Katan 22b). And all the more so according to what was said, that they [Nadav and Avihu] were greater than Moshe and Aharon, all of Israel would be obligated to tear over them." "And His saying <b>and your brothers…</b> <b>shall weep</b> — as a commandment; and if they did not weep, there is no wrath upon them, unlike the law of tearing. And He was precise in saying <b>whom HaShem burned</b> — this may be explained along the lines of what they of blessed memory said (Shabbat 105b), that whoever weeps and mourns over a fitting person, all his sins are forgiven him, and he will weep no more. And this is what He alluded to in saying ״whom HaShem burned״ — meaning, if they weep over the burning, they have only that which has already burned previously — to exclude there being any further burning again. It further intends, in His saying <b>and you shall not die, and upon all</b> etc., that through the death of the righteous, wrath reaches the entirety of Israel, for the strength and vitality of Israel are its great righteous ones, and especially the priests of HaShem who make atonement on behalf of the people."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:53.075627337Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}